window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"60a930f9f4a96ce331c9ed2df2586f9dfdd87df1afde588f97537b3d41f923e0":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.mf06.3\"><\/a>\n\n## 3. Graph regularization of an opposite multiplication vector\n\nThe averages in Section 2 are bounded operators. To connect them with multiplication, we need a regularization that actually lies in the full algebra. Fix \\(s>0\\) and \\(w\\in D\\), and set \\(v=(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w\\). Bounded spectral calculus puts \\(v\\) in \\(D(\\Delta)\\).\n\nFor any \\(v\\in D(S)\\), the opposite version of HA-R3 closes the map \\(z\\mapsto R_zv\\), initially defined on \\(D\\), to an affiliated operator \\(T_v\\). In particular,\n\n\\[\nT_v\\text{ is affiliated with }M,\\qquad\nD\\subseteq D(T_v^*),\\qquad T_v^*z=R_zSv.\n\\tag{MF-G1}\n\\]\n\nWe use this adjoint identity on its stated testing algebra; we do not assert that \\(D\\) is an adjoint core. Let \\(T_v=uh=ku\\) be its two polar decompositions. The positive operators \\(h=(T_v^*T_v)^{1/2}\\), \\(k=(T_vT_v^*)^{1/2}\\) and \\(u\\) have the full polar domains and spectral transport proved in HA-R4. For real \\(f\\in C_c(0,\\infty)\\), extended by zero at zero, HA-R5 gives actual algebra membership and\n\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nf(k)v,\\ f(h)Sv\\in A,\\qquad S(f(k)v)=f(h)Sv,\\\\\n\\lambda_{f(k)v}=kf(k)u,\\qquad\n\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}=hf(h)u^*.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{MF-G2}\n\\]\n\nAll displayed multipliers are bounded extensions. Apply the involution formula also to the real compactly supported function \\(a\\mapsto a^2f(a)^2\\). Since \\(\\Delta v=FSv\\), the anti-linear adjoint pairing gives the nonnegative cross term\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle kf(k)\\Delta v,kf(k)v\\rangle\n&=\\langle\\Delta v,k^2f(k)^2v\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle h^2f(h)^2Sv,Sv\\rangle\n=\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G3}\n\\]\n\nBoth adjoint-domain vectors are legitimate: \\(Sv\\in D(F)\\) because \\(v\\in D(\\Delta)\\), and the cutoff vector lies in \\(A\\subset D(S)\\). If \\(B=kf(k)\\), Cauchy–Schwarz yields\n\\(\\|B\\Delta v\\|^2+s^2\\|Bv\\|^2\\geq2s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\\).\nThe cross term in \\(\\|B(\\Delta+s)v\\|^2\\) has the same nonnegative sign. Polar transport, (MF-G2) and mixed multiplication therefore give\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n4s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\n&\\leq\\|kf(k)w\\|^2\\\\\n&=\\|u\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}w\\|^2\n\\leq\\|R_w\\|^2\\|f(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G4}\n\\]\n\nFor the spectral measure \\(\\mu\\) of \\(h\\) at \\(Sv\\), put \\(c=\\|R_w\\|/(2\\sqrt s)\\). The inequality says\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty(a^2-c^2)f(a)^2\\,d\\mu(a)\\leq0.\n\\tag{MF-G5}\n\\]\n\nA nonnegative cutoff supported in \\((c,\\infty)\\) and equal to one on a prescribed compact subinterval shows that subinterval has zero measure. Countably many such intervals exhaust \\((c,\\infty)\\). Thus \\(P=1_{[0,c]}(h)\\) satisfies \\(PSv=Sv\\); the possible zero spectral mass is retained.\n\nSince \\(P\\in M\\), it commutes with every \\(R_z\\), \\(z\\in D\\). Equation (MF-G1) gives\n\\(R_zSv=PR_zSv=PT_v^*z=Phu^*z\\).\nThe last operator is bounded by \\(c\\). Hence \\(Sv\\) is left bounded, lies in \\(D(S)\\) by the closed-involution property, and belongs to \\(A\\) by fullness. Applying \\(S\\) once more gives \\(v\\in A\\), with the same multiplier norm. We have proved\n\n\\[\n(\\Delta+s)^{-1}D\\subseteq A,\\qquad\n\\|\\lambda_{(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w}\\|\\leq\\frac{\\|R_w\\|}{2\\sqrt s}.\n\\tag{MF-G6}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument for \\(D^{\\mathrm{op}}\\) proves the reciprocal estimate, exchanging \\(\\Delta,A,D,\\lambda,R\\) with \\(\\Delta^{-1},D,A,R,\\lambda\\). No step commutes \\(\\Delta\\) with the unrelated polar operator \\(h\\) or \\(k\\).","html":"<h2>3. Graph regularization of an opposite multiplication vector<\/h2>\n<p>The averages in Section 2 are bounded operators. To connect them with multiplication, we need a regularization that actually lies in the full algebra. Fix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s&gt;0\\)\">\\(s&gt;0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w\\in D\\)\">\\(w\\in D\\)<\/span>, and set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w\\)\">\\(v=(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w\\)<\/span>. Bounded spectral calculus puts <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\)\">\\(v\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(D(\\Delta)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in D(S)\\)\">\\(v\\in D(S)\\)<\/span>, the opposite version of HA-R3 closes the map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\mapsto R_zv\\)\">\\(z\\mapsto R_zv\\)<\/span>, initially defined on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, to an affiliated operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_v\\)\">\\(T_v\\)<\/span>. In particular,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nT_v\\text{ is affiliated with }M,\\qquad\nD\\subseteq D(T_v^*),\\qquad T_v^*z=R_zSv.\n\\tag{MF-G1}\n\\]\">\\[\nT_v\\text{ is affiliated with }M,\\qquad\nD\\subseteq D(T_v^*),\\qquad T_v^*z=R_zSv.\n\\tag{MF-G1}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>We use this adjoint identity on its stated testing algebra; we do not assert that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is an adjoint core. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_v=uh=ku\\)\">\\(T_v=uh=ku\\)<\/span> be its two polar decompositions. The positive operators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=(T_v^*T_v)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(h=(T_v^*T_v)^{1/2}\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=(T_vT_v^*)^{1/2}\\)\">\\(k=(T_vT_v^*)^{1/2}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> have the full polar domains and spectral transport proved in HA-R4. For real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in C_c(0,\\infty)\\)\">\\(f\\in C_c(0,\\infty)\\)<\/span>, extended by zero at zero, HA-R5 gives actual algebra membership and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nf(k)v,\\ f(h)Sv\\in A,\\qquad S(f(k)v)=f(h)Sv,\\\\\n\\lambda_{f(k)v}=kf(k)u,\\qquad\n\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}=hf(h)u^*.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{MF-G2}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nf(k)v,\\ f(h)Sv\\in A,\\qquad S(f(k)v)=f(h)Sv,\\\\\n\\lambda_{f(k)v}=kf(k)u,\\qquad\n\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}=hf(h)u^*.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{MF-G2}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>All displayed multipliers are bounded extensions. Apply the involution formula also to the real compactly supported function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mapsto a^2f(a)^2\\)\">\\(a\\mapsto a^2f(a)^2\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta v=FSv\\)\">\\(\\Delta v=FSv\\)<\/span>, the anti-linear adjoint pairing gives the nonnegative cross term<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle kf(k)\\Delta v,kf(k)v\\rangle\n&amp;=\\langle\\Delta v,k^2f(k)^2v\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle h^2f(h)^2Sv,Sv\\rangle\n=\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G3}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle kf(k)\\Delta v,kf(k)v\\rangle\n&amp;=\\langle\\Delta v,k^2f(k)^2v\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle h^2f(h)^2Sv,Sv\\rangle\n=\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G3}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Both adjoint-domain vectors are legitimate: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Sv\\in D(F)\\)\">\\(Sv\\in D(F)\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in D(\\Delta)\\)\">\\(v\\in D(\\Delta)\\)<\/span>, and the cutoff vector lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\subset D(S)\\)\">\\(A\\subset D(S)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=kf(k)\\)\">\\(B=kf(k)\\)<\/span>, Cauchy–Schwarz yields\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|B\\Delta v\\|^2+s^2\\|Bv\\|^2\\geq2s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|B\\Delta v\\|^2+s^2\\|Bv\\|^2\\geq2s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\\)<\/span>.\nThe cross term in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|B(\\Delta+s)v\\|^2\\)\">\\(\\|B(\\Delta+s)v\\|^2\\)<\/span> has the same nonnegative sign. Polar transport, (MF-G2) and mixed multiplication therefore give<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n4s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\n&amp;\\leq\\|kf(k)w\\|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\\|u\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}w\\|^2\n\\leq\\|R_w\\|^2\\|f(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G4}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n4s\\|hf(h)Sv\\|^2\n&amp;\\leq\\|kf(k)w\\|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\\|u\\lambda_{f(h)Sv}w\\|^2\n\\leq\\|R_w\\|^2\\|f(h)Sv\\|^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{MF-G4}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For the spectral measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu\\)\">\\(\\mu\\)<\/span> of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Sv\\)\">\\(Sv\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=\\|R_w\\|/(2\\sqrt s)\\)\">\\(c=\\|R_w\\|/(2\\sqrt s)\\)<\/span>. The inequality says<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty(a^2-c^2)f(a)^2\\,d\\mu(a)\\leq0.\n\\tag{MF-G5}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\int_0^\\infty(a^2-c^2)f(a)^2\\,d\\mu(a)\\leq0.\n\\tag{MF-G5}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>A nonnegative cutoff supported in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((c,\\infty)\\)\">\\((c,\\infty)\\)<\/span> and equal to one on a prescribed compact subinterval shows that subinterval has zero measure. Countably many such intervals exhaust <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((c,\\infty)\\)\">\\((c,\\infty)\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P=1_{[0,c]}(h)\\)\">\\(P=1_{[0,c]}(h)\\)<\/span> satisfies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(PSv=Sv\\)\">\\(PSv=Sv\\)<\/span>; the possible zero spectral mass is retained.<\/p>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\in M\\)\">\\(P\\in M\\)<\/span>, it commutes with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_z\\)\">\\(R_z\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in D\\)\">\\(z\\in D\\)<\/span>. Equation (MF-G1) gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_zSv=PR_zSv=PT_v^*z=Phu^*z\\)\">\\(R_zSv=PR_zSv=PT_v^*z=Phu^*z\\)<\/span>.\nThe last operator is bounded by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Sv\\)\">\\(Sv\\)<\/span> is left bounded, lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(S)\\)\">\\(D(S)\\)<\/span> by the closed-involution property, and belongs to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> by fullness. Applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> once more gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v\\in A\\)\">\\(v\\in A\\)<\/span>, with the same multiplier norm. We have proved<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n(\\Delta+s)^{-1}D\\subseteq A,\\qquad\n\\|\\lambda_{(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w}\\|\\leq\\frac{\\|R_w\\|}{2\\sqrt s}.\n\\tag{MF-G6}\n\\]\">\\[\n(\\Delta+s)^{-1}D\\subseteq A,\\qquad\n\\|\\lambda_{(\\Delta+s)^{-1}w}\\|\\leq\\frac{\\|R_w\\|}{2\\sqrt s}.\n\\tag{MF-G6}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The same argument for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D^{\\mathrm{op}}\\)\">\\(D^{\\mathrm{op}}\\)<\/span> proves the reciprocal estimate, exchanging <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta,A,D,\\lambda,R\\)\">\\(\\Delta,A,D,\\lambda,R\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta^{-1},D,A,R,\\lambda\\)\">\\(\\Delta^{-1},D,A,R,\\lambda\\)<\/span>. No step commutes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span> with the unrelated polar operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MF06.md","start_offset":11950,"end_offset":15208,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"6046cc61e43a3001043d329ff6c1514a79e28ccbe53dbe29c7a8af6b89b79750":{"markdown":"## BC-6. A noncommuting pair of densities shows the cocycle twist exactly\n\n![Three exact two-by-two matrices: the combined-time cocycle, the equal twisted product, and the different ordinary product](../assets/balanced-cocycle/assets/balanced-cocycle.png)\n\nThe figure illustrates [BC-4, equations BC18–BC20](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.4). Its [plotting source](../assets/balanced-cocycle/render_balanced_cocycle.py) retains the matrices and timing constants, and its [numerical check](../assets/balanced-cocycle/balanced-cocycle-numerics.json) distinguishes floating-point residuals from the exact algebra below. Each cell contains the actual complex entry; shading records only its modulus.\n\nLet \\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\), with faithful states\n\\[\n\\varphi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\varphi x),\\qquad\n\\psi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\psi x),\\qquad\nd_\\varphi=\\frac15\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&4\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\nR=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nd_\\psi=Rd_\\varphi R^*\n=\\frac1{10}\\begin{pmatrix}5&-3\\\\-3&5\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nBoth densities are positive definite with trace one; they do not commute.\n\nFor any positive definite matrix \\(d\\), the weighted-trace GNS model\n\\(\\Lambda(x)=xd^{1/2}\\) in the Hilbert–Schmidt matrix space has\n\\[\nS(X)=d^{-1/2}X^*d^{1/2},\\quad\nJ(X)=X^*,\\quad\n\\Delta^{1/2}(X)=d^{1/2}Xd^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nConjugating by a unitary diagonalizing \\(d\\) verifies that the last map is positive in the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, with eigenvalues \\(\\sqrt{d_i/d_j}\\); direct multiplication gives \\(J\\Delta^{1/2}=S\\). Thus \\(\\sigma_t(x)=d^{it}xd^{-it}\\).\n\nApply this to the balanced weight on \\(M_2(M_2)\\), whose density is the positive definite block matrix \\(\\operatorname{diag}(d_\\varphi,d_\\psi)\\). Its lower-left scalar matrix unit is sent to a block with sole entry \\(d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}\\). Hence BC18 in this example is exactly\n\\[\nu_t=d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}.\n\\]\nDirect multiplication already checks the full twisted law:\n\\[\nu_s\\sigma_s^\\varphi(u_t)\n=d_\\psi^{is}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n d_\\varphi^{is}d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n=d_\\psi^{i(s+t)}d_\\varphi^{-i(s+t)}=u_{s+t}.\n\\]\nOnly powers of the same density are combined; the two densities are not commuted past each other.\n\nPut \\(q=\\log4\\), \\(\\tau=\\pi/(2q)\\). Common scalar phases cancel in \\(u_t\\), and\n\\[\nu_t=R\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&e^{itq}\\end{pmatrix}\nR^*\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&e^{-itq}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAt \\(t=\\tau\\) and \\(2\\tau\\), this gives\n\\[\nu_\\tau=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}1+i&-1-i\\\\1-i&1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nu_{2\\tau}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nOrdinary multiplication instead gives\n\\[\nu_\\tau^2=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}-1+i&-1-i\\\\1-i&-1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nso the exact difference is\n\\[\nu_{2\\tau}-u_\\tau^2\n=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1-i&-1+i\\\\1+i&1+i\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe two columns in this last matrix are orthonormal: each squared norm is one and their inner product is zero. Thus the difference is unitary and its operator norm is exactly one. The twisted product has exactly zero error by the preceding identity. These are the three matrices and the two error norms displayed.\n\nThe example demonstrates why the automorphism in BC20 is necessary; it is not a proof of the arbitrary-weight theorem or a statement that every cocycle has this finite-matrix form. The complete general proof is [BC-1](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.1)–5. For free author context see [Hiai, §7.2, printed pp.65–67](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.02383v1#page=65). This independent matrix calculation, figure and source are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.\n\n[Editable original SVG](../assets/balanced-cocycle/assets/balanced-cocycle.svg).","html":"<h2>BC-6. A noncommuting pair of densities shows the cocycle twist exactly<\/h2>\n<p><a class=\"figure-link\" href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/balanced-cocycle/assets/balanced-cocycle.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img src=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/balanced-cocycle/assets/balanced-cocycle.png\" alt=\"Three exact two-by-two matrices: the combined-time cocycle, the equal twisted product, and the different ordinary product\" /><span>Open figure at full size<\/span><\/a><\/p>\n<p>The figure illustrates <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.4\">BC-4, equations BC18–BC20<\/a>. Its <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/balanced-cocycle/render_balanced_cocycle.py\">plotting source<\/a> retains the matrices and timing constants, and its <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/balanced-cocycle/balanced-cocycle-numerics.json\">numerical check<\/a> distinguishes floating-point residuals from the exact algebra below. Each cell contains the actual complex entry; shading records only its modulus.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(M=M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>, with faithful states\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\varphi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\varphi x),\\qquad\n\\psi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\psi x),\\qquad\nd_\\varphi=\\frac15\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;4\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\">\\[\n\\varphi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\varphi x),\\qquad\n\\psi(x)=\\operatorname{Tr}(d_\\psi x),\\qquad\nd_\\varphi=\\frac15\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;4\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]<\/span>\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nR=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nd_\\psi=Rd_\\varphi R^*\n=\\frac1{10}\\begin{pmatrix}5&amp;-3\\\\-3&amp;5\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\">\\[\nR=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;1\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nd_\\psi=Rd_\\varphi R^*\n=\\frac1{10}\\begin{pmatrix}5&amp;-3\\\\-3&amp;5\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth densities are positive definite with trace one; they do not commute.<\/p>\n<p>For any positive definite matrix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>, the weighted-trace GNS model\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda(x)=xd^{1/2}\\)\">\\(\\Lambda(x)=xd^{1/2}\\)<\/span> in the Hilbert–Schmidt matrix space has\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nS(X)=d^{-1/2}X^*d^{1/2},\\quad\nJ(X)=X^*,\\quad\n\\Delta^{1/2}(X)=d^{1/2}Xd^{-1/2}.\n\\]\">\\[\nS(X)=d^{-1/2}X^*d^{1/2},\\quad\nJ(X)=X^*,\\quad\n\\Delta^{1/2}(X)=d^{1/2}Xd^{-1/2}.\n\\]<\/span>\nConjugating by a unitary diagonalizing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> verifies that the last map is positive in the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product, with eigenvalues <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sqrt{d_i/d_j}\\)\">\\(\\sqrt{d_i/d_j}\\)<\/span>; direct multiplication gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\Delta^{1/2}=S\\)\">\\(J\\Delta^{1/2}=S\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t(x)=d^{it}xd^{-it}\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t(x)=d^{it}xd^{-it}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Apply this to the balanced weight on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(M_2)\\)\">\\(M_2(M_2)\\)<\/span>, whose density is the positive definite block matrix <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{diag}(d_\\varphi,d_\\psi)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{diag}(d_\\varphi,d_\\psi)\\)<\/span>. Its lower-left scalar matrix unit is sent to a block with sole entry <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}\\)\">\\(d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}\\)<\/span>. Hence BC18 in this example is exactly\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_t=d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}.\n\\]\">\\[\nu_t=d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}.\n\\]<\/span>\nDirect multiplication already checks the full twisted law:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_s\\sigma_s^\\varphi(u_t)\n=d_\\psi^{is}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n d_\\varphi^{is}d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n=d_\\psi^{i(s+t)}d_\\varphi^{-i(s+t)}=u_{s+t}.\n\\]\">\\[\nu_s\\sigma_s^\\varphi(u_t)\n=d_\\psi^{is}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n d_\\varphi^{is}d_\\psi^{it}d_\\varphi^{-it}d_\\varphi^{-is}\n=d_\\psi^{i(s+t)}d_\\varphi^{-i(s+t)}=u_{s+t}.\n\\]<\/span>\nOnly powers of the same density are combined; the two densities are not commuted past each other.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=\\log4\\)\">\\(q=\\log4\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau=\\pi/(2q)\\)\">\\(\\tau=\\pi/(2q)\\)<\/span>. Common scalar phases cancel in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u_t\\)\">\\(u_t\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_t=R\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{itq}\\end{pmatrix}\nR^*\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{-itq}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\">\\[\nu_t=R\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{itq}\\end{pmatrix}\nR^*\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{-itq}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]<\/span>\nAt <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=\\tau\\)\">\\(t=\\tau\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2\\tau\\)\">\\(2\\tau\\)<\/span>, this gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_\\tau=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}1+i&amp;-1-i\\\\1-i&amp;1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nu_{2\\tau}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\">\\[\nu_\\tau=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}1+i&amp;-1-i\\\\1-i&amp;1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nu_{2\\tau}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]<\/span>\nOrdinary multiplication instead gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_\\tau^2=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}-1+i&amp;-1-i\\\\1-i&amp;-1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\">\\[\nu_\\tau^2=\\frac12\n\\begin{pmatrix}-1+i&amp;-1-i\\\\1-i&amp;-1-i\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]<\/span>\nso the exact difference is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu_{2\\tau}-u_\\tau^2\n=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1-i&amp;-1+i\\\\1+i&amp;1+i\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\">\\[\nu_{2\\tau}-u_\\tau^2\n=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1-i&amp;-1+i\\\\1+i&amp;1+i\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe two columns in this last matrix are orthonormal: each squared norm is one and their inner product is zero. Thus the difference is unitary and its operator norm is exactly one. The twisted product has exactly zero error by the preceding identity. These are the three matrices and the two error norms displayed.<\/p>\n<p>The example demonstrates why the automorphism in BC20 is necessary; it is not a proof of the arbitrary-weight theorem or a statement that every cocycle has this finite-matrix form. The complete general proof is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.1\">BC-1<\/a>–5. For free author context see <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.02383v1#page=65\">Hiai, §7.2, printed pp.65–67<\/a>. This independent matrix calculation, figure and source are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held.<\/p>\n<p><a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/assets/balanced-cocycle/assets/balanced-cocycle.svg\">Editable original SVG<\/a>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-BC.md","start_offset":17614,"end_offset":21236,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"60162a58a5def6055801319aff2eb9058d8e4b1a029bcdd14ef54729a98d3353":{"markdown":"## FD-1. Restriction commutes with the entire scalar extension\n\nWe need a restriction statement before knowing that the restriction is semifinite. Let \\(\\rho\\) be any normal weight on \\(B\\), and let \\(\\chi=\\rho|_{F_+}\\). This is a normal weight: bounded increasing positive nets in the concrete von Neumann subalgebra have the same strong supremum in \\(F\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nFor \\(m\\in\\widehat F_+\\), write its complete spectral pair as \\((e,A)\\), with infinite part \\(1-e\\). The canonical bounded approximants in [EP-2](OA-FLOW-EP.md#oa-flow.ep.2)/[EP-3](OA-FLOW-EP.md#oa-flow.ep.3) are\n\n<a id=\"equation-fd7\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n a_k=(A\\wedge k)e+k(1-e)\\in F_+,\\qquad a_k\\uparrow m.\n \\tag{FD7}\n\\]\nTheir quadratic forms and their values on all positive normal functionals also increase to those of \\(\\iota(m)\\) in \\(B\\): [CC-4](OA-FLOW-CC.md#oa-flow.cc.4) proves that this inclusion retains the spectral pair. [EP-5](OA-FLOW-EP.md#oa-flow.ep.5) therefore gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-fd8\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\widehat\\chi(m)\n =\\sup_k\\chi(a_k)=\\sup_k\\rho(a_k)\n =\\widehat\\rho(\\iota(m)).\n \\tag{FD8}\n\\]\nEvery equality includes infinity. No semifiniteness, normal-weight sum theorem, directedness of the dominated functional family, or surjectivity assertion about that family is needed. We will use this identity with a bounded-density weight \\(\\rho=\\tau_d\\).\n\n<a id=\"fd-2\"><\/a><a id=\"oa-flow.fd.2\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>FD-1. Restriction commutes with the entire scalar extension<\/h2>\n<p>We need a restriction statement before knowing that the restriction is semifinite. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho\\)\">\\(\\rho\\)<\/span> be any normal weight on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi=\\rho|_{F_+}\\)\">\\(\\chi=\\rho|_{F_+}\\)<\/span>. This is a normal weight: bounded increasing positive nets in the concrete von Neumann subalgebra have the same strong supremum in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\in\\widehat F_+\\)\">\\(m\\in\\widehat F_+\\)<\/span>, write its complete spectral pair as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((e,A)\\)\">\\((e,A)\\)<\/span>, with infinite part <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-e\\)\">\\(1-e\\)<\/span>. The canonical bounded approximants in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EP.html#oa-flow.ep.2\">EP-2<\/a>/<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EP.html#oa-flow.ep.3\">EP-3<\/a> are<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n a_k=(A\\wedge k)e+k(1-e)\\in F_+,\\qquad a_k\\uparrow m.\n \\tag{FD7}\n\\]\">\\[\n a_k=(A\\wedge k)e+k(1-e)\\in F_+,\\qquad a_k\\uparrow m.\n \\tag{FD7}\n\\]<\/span>\nTheir quadratic forms and their values on all positive normal functionals also increase to those of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\iota(m)\\)\">\\(\\iota(m)\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>: <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CC.html#oa-flow.cc.4\">CC-4<\/a> proves that this inclusion retains the spectral pair. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-EP.html#oa-flow.ep.5\">EP-5<\/a> therefore gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat\\chi(m)\n =\\sup_k\\chi(a_k)=\\sup_k\\rho(a_k)\n =\\widehat\\rho(\\iota(m)).\n \\tag{FD8}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat\\chi(m)\n =\\sup_k\\chi(a_k)=\\sup_k\\rho(a_k)\n =\\widehat\\rho(\\iota(m)).\n \\tag{FD8}\n\\]<\/span>\nEvery equality includes infinity. No semifiniteness, normal-weight sum theorem, directedness of the dominated functional family, or surjectivity assertion about that family is needed. We will use this identity with a bounded-density weight <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\rho=\\tau_d\\)\">\\(\\rho=\\tau_d\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-FD.md","start_offset":4765,"end_offset":6131,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"60fafad616c10b29c1b91823feea7dff2bb201a3d4f6435828d0cc8ff8a6d78b":{"markdown":"## BS3. Hull spectra and exact filter laws\n\nDefine closed convolution ideals and their character hulls by\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs14\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n I_x^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha x=0\\},\\qquad\n I^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha=0\\}=\\bigcap_x I_x^\\alpha,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=h(I_x^\\alpha),\\quad\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=h(I^\\alpha).\n \\tag{BS14}\n\\]\nClosedness and the ideal property follow from (BS9)–(BS10). LF6–7 apply to these ideals. Thus \\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)\\) are closed, \\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(0)=\\varnothing\\), and\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs15\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ near }\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\n   \\Longrightarrow T_f^\\alpha x=0,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=\\varnothing\\Longrightarrow x=0.\n \\tag{BS15}\n\\]\nThe second assertion uses BS2's essentiality after LF6's empty-hull conclusion.\n\nFor \\(f\\in L^1(G)\\),\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs16\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\{\\widehat f\\ne0\\}\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(T_f^\\alpha x)\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f.\n \\tag{BS16}\n\\]\nThe first part of the right inclusion follows because \\(I_x^\\alpha\\subset I_{T_fx}^\\alpha\\). If \\(\\gamma\\notin\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f\\), LF1 supplies \\(g\\) with \\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=1\\) and compact support where \\(\\widehat f=0\\). Fourier injectivity gives \\(g*f=0\\), so \\(T_gT_fx=0\\) and excludes \\(\\gamma\\). For the left inclusion, \\(T_gT_fx=0\\) implies \\(g*f\\in I_x^\\alpha\\); at \\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\) with \\(\\widehat f(\\gamma)\\ne0\\), this forces \\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=0\\) for every such \\(g\\).\n\nIf \\(\\widehat f=1\\) on a neighbourhood of \\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\), then\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs17\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n T_f^\\alpha x=x.\n \\tag{BS17}\n\\]\nIndeed for every \\(g\\), the transform of \\(g-g*f\\) vanishes on that neighbourhood, so (BS15) gives \\(T_g(x-T_fx)=0\\); use BS2. Applying (BS16) to (BS13) proves the promised bounded compact-spectrum core:\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs18\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n x_i=T_{k_i}^\\alpha x\\in X_\\alpha(K_i),\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x_i)\\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap K_i,\n \\quad x_i\\to x\\text{ in }\\tau,\\quad \\|x_i\\|\\leq C_\\alpha\\|x\\|.\n \\tag{BS18}\n\\]\nHere, for every subset \\(E\\subset\\Gamma\\), define \\(X_\\alpha(E)=\\{x:\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\subset E\\}\\), without replacing \\(E\\) by its closure.\n\nEach \\(X_\\alpha(E)\\) is linear. For example, if \\(\\gamma\\) is outside both \\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(z)\\), choose annihilators \\(f,g\\) nonzero at \\(\\gamma\\); their convolution annihilates both \\(x,z\\) and remains nonzero there. This gives the spectrum union bound for sums. Scalar multiplication is immediate. Arbitrary intersections satisfy \\(X_\\alpha(\\bigcap_jE_j)=\\bigcap_jX_\\alpha(E_j)\\), including the empty family. Covariance and invertibility give \\(I_{\\alpha_sx}^\\alpha=I_x^\\alpha\\), hence invariance. For closed \\(E\\), LF7 gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs19\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n X_\\alpha(E)=\\bigcap_{\\widehat f=0\\text{ near }E}\\ker T_f^\\alpha,\n \\tag{BS19}\n\\]\nso it is \\(\\tau\\)-closed. No closedness is claimed for an arbitrary nonclosed \\(E\\).\n\nFinally\n\n<a id=\"equation-bs20\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=\n \\overline{\\bigcup_{x\\in X}\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)}.\n \\tag{BS20}\n\\]\nOne inclusion follows from \\(I^\\alpha\\subset I_x^\\alpha\\). If \\(\\gamma\\) is outside the closed union, LF1 supplies \\(f\\) nonzero at \\(\\gamma\\) with compact Fourier support disjoint from that union. Its transform vanishes near every vector spectrum, so (BS15) gives \\(T_fx=0\\) for every \\(x\\), excluding \\(\\gamma\\) from \\(h(I^\\alpha)\\). A nonzero \\(X\\) therefore has nonempty action spectrum.\n\n<a id=\"bs-4\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>BS3. Hull spectra and exact filter laws<\/h2>\n<p>Define closed convolution ideals and their character hulls by<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n I_x^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha x=0\\},\\qquad\n I^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha=0\\}=\\bigcap_x I_x^\\alpha,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=h(I_x^\\alpha),\\quad\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=h(I^\\alpha).\n \\tag{BS14}\n\\]\">\\[\n I_x^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha x=0\\},\\qquad\n I^\\alpha=\\{f:T_f^\\alpha=0\\}=\\bigcap_x I_x^\\alpha,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=h(I_x^\\alpha),\\quad\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=h(I^\\alpha).\n \\tag{BS14}\n\\]<\/span>\nClosedness and the ideal property follow from (BS9)–(BS10). LF6–7 apply to these ideals. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)\\)<\/span> are closed, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(0)=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(0)=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>, and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ near }\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\n   \\Longrightarrow T_f^\\alpha x=0,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=\\varnothing\\Longrightarrow x=0.\n \\tag{BS15}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widehat f=0\\text{ near }\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\n   \\Longrightarrow T_f^\\alpha x=0,\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)=\\varnothing\\Longrightarrow x=0.\n \\tag{BS15}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe second assertion uses BS2's essentiality after LF6's empty-hull conclusion.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in L^1(G)\\)\">\\(f\\in L^1(G)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\{\\widehat f\\ne0\\}\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(T_f^\\alpha x)\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f.\n \\tag{BS16}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\{\\widehat f\\ne0\\}\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(T_f^\\alpha x)\n \\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f.\n \\tag{BS16}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe first part of the right inclusion follows because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_x^\\alpha\\subset I_{T_fx}^\\alpha\\)\">\\(I_x^\\alpha\\subset I_{T_fx}^\\alpha\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\notin\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\notin\\operatorname{supp}\\widehat f\\)<\/span>, LF1 supplies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=1\\)\">\\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=1\\)<\/span> and compact support where <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat f=0\\)\">\\(\\widehat f=0\\)<\/span>. Fourier injectivity gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g*f=0\\)\">\\(g*f=0\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_gT_fx=0\\)\">\\(T_gT_fx=0\\)<\/span> and excludes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span>. For the left inclusion, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_gT_fx=0\\)\">\\(T_gT_fx=0\\)<\/span> implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g*f\\in I_x^\\alpha\\)\">\\(g*f\\in I_x^\\alpha\\)<\/span>; at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat f(\\gamma)\\ne0\\)\">\\(\\widehat f(\\gamma)\\ne0\\)<\/span>, this forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=0\\)\">\\(\\widehat g(\\gamma)=0\\)<\/span> for every such <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat f=1\\)\">\\(\\widehat f=1\\)<\/span> on a neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)<\/span>, then<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_f^\\alpha x=x.\n \\tag{BS17}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_f^\\alpha x=x.\n \\tag{BS17}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, the transform of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g-g*f\\)\">\\(g-g*f\\)<\/span> vanishes on that neighbourhood, so (BS15) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_g(x-T_fx)=0\\)\">\\(T_g(x-T_fx)=0\\)<\/span>; use BS2. Applying (BS16) to (BS13) proves the promised bounded compact-spectrum core:<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n x_i=T_{k_i}^\\alpha x\\in X_\\alpha(K_i),\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x_i)\\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap K_i,\n \\quad x_i\\to x\\text{ in }\\tau,\\quad \\|x_i\\|\\leq C_\\alpha\\|x\\|.\n \\tag{BS18}\n\\]\">\\[\n x_i=T_{k_i}^\\alpha x\\in X_\\alpha(K_i),\\qquad\n \\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x_i)\\subset\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\cap K_i,\n \\quad x_i\\to x\\text{ in }\\tau,\\quad \\|x_i\\|\\leq C_\\alpha\\|x\\|.\n \\tag{BS18}\n\\]<\/span>\nHere, for every subset <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\subset\\Gamma\\)\">\\(E\\subset\\Gamma\\)<\/span>, define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_\\alpha(E)=\\{x:\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\subset E\\}\\)\">\\(X_\\alpha(E)=\\{x:\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\subset E\\}\\)<\/span>, without replacing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> by its closure.<\/p>\n<p>Each <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_\\alpha(E)\\)\">\\(X_\\alpha(E)\\)<\/span> is linear. For example, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span> is outside both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(z)\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(z)\\)<\/span>, choose annihilators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f,g\\)\">\\(f,g\\)<\/span> nonzero at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span>; their convolution annihilates both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x,z\\)\">\\(x,z\\)<\/span> and remains nonzero there. This gives the spectrum union bound for sums. Scalar multiplication is immediate. Arbitrary intersections satisfy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X_\\alpha(\\bigcap_jE_j)=\\bigcap_jX_\\alpha(E_j)\\)\">\\(X_\\alpha(\\bigcap_jE_j)=\\bigcap_jX_\\alpha(E_j)\\)<\/span>, including the empty family. Covariance and invertibility give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_{\\alpha_sx}^\\alpha=I_x^\\alpha\\)\">\\(I_{\\alpha_sx}^\\alpha=I_x^\\alpha\\)<\/span>, hence invariance. For closed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, LF7 gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n X_\\alpha(E)=\\bigcap_{\\widehat f=0\\text{ near }E}\\ker T_f^\\alpha,\n \\tag{BS19}\n\\]\">\\[\n X_\\alpha(E)=\\bigcap_{\\widehat f=0\\text{ near }E}\\ker T_f^\\alpha,\n \\tag{BS19}\n\\]<\/span>\nso it is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\)<\/span>-closed. No closedness is claimed for an arbitrary nonclosed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Finally<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=\n \\overline{\\bigcup_{x\\in X}\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)}.\n \\tag{BS20}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\operatorname{sp}(\\alpha)=\n \\overline{\\bigcup_{x\\in X}\\operatorname{sp}_\\alpha(x)}.\n \\tag{BS20}\n\\]<\/span>\nOne inclusion follows from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I^\\alpha\\subset I_x^\\alpha\\)\">\\(I^\\alpha\\subset I_x^\\alpha\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span> is outside the closed union, LF1 supplies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> nonzero at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span> with compact Fourier support disjoint from that union. Its transform vanishes near every vector spectrum, so (BS15) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_fx=0\\)\">\\(T_fx=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, excluding <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\gamma\\)\">\\(\\gamma\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h(I^\\alpha)\\)\">\\(h(I^\\alpha)\\)<\/span>. A nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(X\\)\">\\(X\\)<\/span> therefore has nonempty action spectrum.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-BS.md","start_offset":8544,"end_offset":12199,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"601c7e6b9ba3b55a739f158fc6e31e2408cfe10cdffde48fe9b979fd7d464bd4":{"markdown":"## Resolvents on an ordinary Banach space\n\nLet \\(E,T,A\\) be the norm-continuous-orbit group and full norm generator from [the norm-domain theorem](OA-FLOW-L94.md#oa-flow.gen.norm), with \\(C=\\sup_t\\|T_t\\|<\\infty\\). For \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda>0\\), the vector integral\n\\[\n Q_\\lambda x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|Q_\\lambda\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\n \\tag{NR1}\n\\]\nexists in \\(E\\) by the complete Banach integration theorem. Linearity and its norm bound make \\(Q_\\lambda\\) a bounded operator on all of \\(E\\).\n\nThe translated integral satisfies, for real \\(h\\),\n\\[\n T_hQ_\\lambda x\n =e^{\\lambda h}Q_\\lambda x\n  -e^{\\lambda h}\\int_0^h e^{-\\lambda u}T_ux\\,du .\n \\tag{NR2}\n\\]\nThis follows by the scalar change of variable \\(u=t+h\\), with oriented integration if \\(h<0\\); bounded maps pass through the vector integral. The last integral divided by \\(h\\) converges to \\(x\\) in norm by orbit continuity. Taking the norm derivative at zero gives\n\\[\n Q_\\lambda E\\subset D(A),\\qquad\n (\\lambda I-A)Q_\\lambda x=x\\quad(x\\in E).\n \\tag{NR3}\n\\]\nFor \\(x\\in D(A)\\), (NG3) gives\n\\[\n \\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\bigr)\n   =-e^{-\\lambda t}T_t(\\lambda I-A)x\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NR4}\n\\]\nIntegrating to \\(b>0\\), then letting \\(b\\to\\infty\\), is justified by\n\\(\\|e^{-\\lambda b}T_bx\\|\\leq C e^{-b\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\\|x\\|\\to0\\).\nIt proves \\(Q_\\lambda(\\lambda I-A)x=x\\). Thus both inverse identities hold, on all of \\(E\\) and on the entire domain \\(D(A)\\), respectively.\n\nFor \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda<0\\), apply this proof to \\(t\\mapsto T_{-t}\\), whose norm generator is \\(-A\\) on the same domain, and parameter \\(-\\lambda\\). The result is\n\\[\n (\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x\n =-\\int_0^\\infty e^{\\lambda t}T_{-t}x\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|(\\lambda I-A)^{-1}\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|}.\n \\tag{NR5}\n\\]\nTogether with (NR1)–(NR4), this proves the resolvent assertion on both open half-planes and \\(\\sigma(A)\\subset i\\mathbb R\\), now for an ordinary Banach space with its norm-closed, norm-dense generator domain.\n\nScalar twists have the same full norm domain. For \\(b\\in\\mathbb R\\), put \\(S_t=e^{ibt}T_t\\). For \\(x\\in D(A)\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{S_hx-x}{h}\n =e^{ibh}\\frac{T_hx-x}{h}+\\frac{e^{ibh}-1}{h}x\n \\longrightarrow Ax+ibx .\n\\]\nConversely, the same identity applied to \\(T_t=e^{-ibt}S_t\\) shows that existence of the norm derivative for \\(S\\) implies \\(x\\in D(A)\\). Its generator is therefore \\(A+ibI\\) on exactly \\(D(A)\\), and\n\\[\n (\\lambda I-(A+ibI))^{-1}\n   =((\\lambda-ib)I-A)^{-1}\\qquad(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\ne0).\n\\]\nBoth sides act on all of \\(E\\) with range \\(D(A)\\), by the inverse identities just proved.\n\nIn particular, if \\(T_t\\) are isometries, then for every nonzero real \\(s\\),\n\\[\n (I-sA)^{-1}x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u}T_{su}x\\,du,\\qquad\n \\|(I-sA)^{-1}\\|\\leq1 .\n \\tag{NR6}\n\\]\nFor \\(s>0\\) this is (NR1) with \\(\\lambda=1/s\\) and a positive scaling; for \\(s<0\\) it is (NR5) with the same parameter and the corresponding backward scaling. The inverse has range exactly \\(D(A)\\), by the two inverse identities. No weak-star compactness is used.\n\nFor the \\(c_0\\) model (NG7), these formulas give\n\\[\n ((\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x)_n=\\frac{x_n}{\\lambda-in}.\n \\tag{NR7}\n\\]\nThis sequence is in \\(c_0\\), since its absolute value is at most \\(|x_n|/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\). It lies in the full domain (NG8) because\n\\(n/(\\lambda-in)=i(1-\\lambda/(\\lambda-in))\\) is bounded and \\(x_n\\to0\\).\nThus the coordinate formula verifies the claimed range, as well as the inverse equation.\n\nThe source comparison is Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras II*, XI.1, p.312 and Lemma1.19, pp.325–326. The norm argument above retains both time signs and both domain-sensitive inverse identities.","html":"<h2>Resolvents on an ordinary Banach space<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E,T,A\\)\">\\(E,T,A\\)<\/span> be the norm-continuous-orbit group and full norm generator from <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L94.html#oa-flow.gen.norm\">the norm-domain theorem<\/a>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=\\sup_t\\|T_t\\|&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(C=\\sup_t\\|T_t\\|&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&gt;0\\)<\/span>, the vector integral\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n Q_\\lambda x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|Q_\\lambda\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\n \\tag{NR1}\n\\]\">\\[\n Q_\\lambda x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|Q_\\lambda\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\n \\tag{NR1}\n\\]<\/span>\nexists in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> by the complete Banach integration theorem. Linearity and its norm bound make <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_\\lambda\\)\">\\(Q_\\lambda\\)<\/span> a bounded operator on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The translated integral satisfies, for real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_hQ_\\lambda x\n =e^{\\lambda h}Q_\\lambda x\n  -e^{\\lambda h}\\int_0^h e^{-\\lambda u}T_ux\\,du .\n \\tag{NR2}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_hQ_\\lambda x\n =e^{\\lambda h}Q_\\lambda x\n  -e^{\\lambda h}\\int_0^h e^{-\\lambda u}T_ux\\,du .\n \\tag{NR2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis follows by the scalar change of variable <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u=t+h\\)\">\\(u=t+h\\)<\/span>, with oriented integration if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h&lt;0\\)\">\\(h&lt;0\\)<\/span>; bounded maps pass through the vector integral. The last integral divided by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span> converges to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> in norm by orbit continuity. Taking the norm derivative at zero gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n Q_\\lambda E\\subset D(A),\\qquad\n (\\lambda I-A)Q_\\lambda x=x\\quad(x\\in E).\n \\tag{NR3}\n\\]\">\\[\n Q_\\lambda E\\subset D(A),\\qquad\n (\\lambda I-A)Q_\\lambda x=x\\quad(x\\in E).\n \\tag{NR3}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in D(A)\\)\">\\(x\\in D(A)\\)<\/span>, (NG3) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\bigr)\n   =-e^{-\\lambda t}T_t(\\lambda I-A)x\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NR4}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(e^{-\\lambda t}T_tx\\bigr)\n   =-e^{-\\lambda t}T_t(\\lambda I-A)x\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NR4}\n\\]<\/span>\nIntegrating to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&gt;0\\)\">\\(b&gt;0\\)<\/span>, then letting <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\to\\infty\\)\">\\(b\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>, is justified by\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|e^{-\\lambda b}T_bx\\|\\leq C e^{-b\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\\|x\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|e^{-\\lambda b}T_bx\\|\\leq C e^{-b\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda}\\|x\\|\\to0\\)<\/span>.\nIt proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_\\lambda(\\lambda I-A)x=x\\)\">\\(Q_\\lambda(\\lambda I-A)x=x\\)<\/span>. Thus both inverse identities hold, on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> and on the entire domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(A)\\)\">\\(D(A)\\)<\/span>, respectively.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&lt;0\\)\">\\(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda&lt;0\\)<\/span>, apply this proof to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\mapsto T_{-t}\\)\">\\(t\\mapsto T_{-t}\\)<\/span>, whose norm generator is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-A\\)\">\\(-A\\)<\/span> on the same domain, and parameter <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-\\lambda\\)\">\\(-\\lambda\\)<\/span>. The result is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x\n =-\\int_0^\\infty e^{\\lambda t}T_{-t}x\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|(\\lambda I-A)^{-1}\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|}.\n \\tag{NR5}\n\\]\">\\[\n (\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x\n =-\\int_0^\\infty e^{\\lambda t}T_{-t}x\\,dt,\\qquad\n \\|(\\lambda I-A)^{-1}\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|}.\n \\tag{NR5}\n\\]<\/span>\nTogether with (NR1)–(NR4), this proves the resolvent assertion on both open half-planes and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma(A)\\subset i\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(\\sigma(A)\\subset i\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, now for an ordinary Banach space with its norm-closed, norm-dense generator domain.<\/p>\n<p>Scalar twists have the same full norm domain. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\in\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S_t=e^{ibt}T_t\\)\">\\(S_t=e^{ibt}T_t\\)<\/span>. For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in D(A)\\)\">\\(x\\in D(A)\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\frac{S_hx-x}{h}\n =e^{ibh}\\frac{T_hx-x}{h}+\\frac{e^{ibh}-1}{h}x\n \\longrightarrow Ax+ibx .\n\\]\">\\[\n \\frac{S_hx-x}{h}\n =e^{ibh}\\frac{T_hx-x}{h}+\\frac{e^{ibh}-1}{h}x\n \\longrightarrow Ax+ibx .\n\\]<\/span>\nConversely, the same identity applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t=e^{-ibt}S_t\\)\">\\(T_t=e^{-ibt}S_t\\)<\/span> shows that existence of the norm derivative for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(S\\)\">\\(S\\)<\/span> implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in D(A)\\)\">\\(x\\in D(A)\\)<\/span>. Its generator is therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A+ibI\\)\">\\(A+ibI\\)<\/span> on exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(A)\\)\">\\(D(A)\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (\\lambda I-(A+ibI))^{-1}\n   =((\\lambda-ib)I-A)^{-1}\\qquad(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\ne0).\n\\]\">\\[\n (\\lambda I-(A+ibI))^{-1}\n   =((\\lambda-ib)I-A)^{-1}\\qquad(\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\ne0).\n\\]<\/span>\nBoth sides act on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> with range <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(A)\\)\">\\(D(A)\\)<\/span>, by the inverse identities just proved.<\/p>\n<p>In particular, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t\\)\">\\(T_t\\)<\/span> are isometries, then for every nonzero real <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (I-sA)^{-1}x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u}T_{su}x\\,du,\\qquad\n \\|(I-sA)^{-1}\\|\\leq1 .\n \\tag{NR6}\n\\]\">\\[\n (I-sA)^{-1}x=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-u}T_{su}x\\,du,\\qquad\n \\|(I-sA)^{-1}\\|\\leq1 .\n \\tag{NR6}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s&gt;0\\)\">\\(s&gt;0\\)<\/span> this is (NR1) with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda=1/s\\)\">\\(\\lambda=1/s\\)<\/span> and a positive scaling; for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s&lt;0\\)\">\\(s&lt;0\\)<\/span> it is (NR5) with the same parameter and the corresponding backward scaling. The inverse has range exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D(A)\\)\">\\(D(A)\\)<\/span>, by the two inverse identities. No weak-star compactness is used.<\/p>\n<p>For the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0\\)\">\\(c_0\\)<\/span> model (NG7), these formulas give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n ((\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x)_n=\\frac{x_n}{\\lambda-in}.\n \\tag{NR7}\n\\]\">\\[\n ((\\lambda I-A)^{-1}x)_n=\\frac{x_n}{\\lambda-in}.\n \\tag{NR7}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis sequence is in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_0\\)\">\\(c_0\\)<\/span>, since its absolute value is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|x_n|/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\)\">\\(|x_n|/|\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda|\\)<\/span>. It lies in the full domain (NG8) because\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n/(\\lambda-in)=i(1-\\lambda/(\\lambda-in))\\)\">\\(n/(\\lambda-in)=i(1-\\lambda/(\\lambda-in))\\)<\/span> is bounded and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n\\to0\\)\">\\(x_n\\to0\\)<\/span>.\nThus the coordinate formula verifies the claimed range, as well as the inverse equation.<\/p>\n<p>The source comparison is Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, XI.1, p.312 and Lemma1.19, pp.325–326. The norm argument above retains both time signs and both domain-sensitive inverse identities.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L95.md","start_offset":6866,"end_offset":10551,"role":"complete_registered_reader_section"},"606ca2a2f2cc64aafdf39d2613a417f7cff2db5016795273b9d9dc86d071bb46":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.cstd.basechange\"><\/a>\n## Changing the reference probability\n\nThe Hilbert multiplier is forced by the chosen measure, while the fibre arrows describe the action independently of that choice. This can be checked without reconstructing any field.\n\nLet \\(r:X\\to(0,\\infty)\\) be measurable with \\(\\int r\\,d\\mu=1\\), and put \\(d\\mu'=r\\,d\\mu\\). Values on a null set can be chosen arbitrarily. The map\n\\[\n W:\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\n          \\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu',\n \\qquad (W\\eta)(x)=r(x)^{-1/2}\\eta(x)\n \\tag{B1}\n\\]\nis unitary: integration of its squared norm cancels the factor \\(r\\), and multiplication by \\(r^{1/2}\\) is its inverse. No boundedness of \\(r\\) or \\(r^{-1}\\) is needed. The formula itself gives the exact square-integrable domains.\n\nFor each fixed \\(g\\), testing against bounded nonnegative Borel \\(f\\) gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_X f(y)\\,d(T_g)_*\\mu'(y)\n &=\\int_X f(T_gx)r(x)\\,d\\mu(x)\\\\\n &=\\int_X f(y)\\,r(T_g^{-1}y)j_g(y)\\,d\\mu(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe scalar Radon–Nikodym uniqueness proved in the\n[finite density theorem](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation)\ntherefore yields\n\\[\n j'_g(y)=j_g(y)\\frac{r(T_g^{-1}y)}{r(y)}\n \\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n \\tag{B2}\n\\]\nSubstituting (B1) into (T4) shows\n\\[\n (WU_gW^{-1}\\zeta)(y)\n   =j'_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\zeta(T_g^{-1}y).\n \\tag{B3}\n\\]\nThe **same** fibre arrows work in the new probability coordinates. Since \\(r^{-1/2}\\) is real and positive, \\(W\\) also intertwines the conjugations, the cones and every decomposable algebra element. Essential boundedness and equality of fields are unchanged because \\(\\mu'\\sim\\mu\\).\n\nFor a positive normal functional \\(\\psi\\), its representing section in the new Hilbert coordinates is \\(r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi(x)\\). Its central mass and its normalized conditional vector obey\n\\[\n h'_\\psi=\\frac{h_\\psi}{r},\\qquad\n (h'_\\psi)^{-1/2}r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n       =h_\\psi^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n \\quad\\text{where }h_\\psi>0.\n \\tag{B4}\n\\]\nThus changing the reference probability changes both the raw section and its mass, but their normalized cone vector is unchanged. This includes using another faithful normal state as the reference: its restriction to \\(D\\) is an equivalent probability, and its own conditional states have their cone vectors in these same standard-form fibres.","html":"<h2>Changing the reference probability<\/h2>\n<p>The Hilbert multiplier is forced by the chosen measure, while the fibre arrows describe the action independently of that choice. This can be checked without reconstructing any field.<\/p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r:X\\to(0,\\infty)\\)\">\\(r:X\\to(0,\\infty)\\)<\/span> be measurable with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int r\\,d\\mu=1\\)\">\\(\\int r\\,d\\mu=1\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\mu&#x27;=r\\,d\\mu\\)\">\\(d\\mu&#x27;=r\\,d\\mu\\)<\/span>. Values on a null set can be chosen arbitrarily. The map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n W:\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\n          \\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu&#x27;,\n \\qquad (W\\eta)(x)=r(x)^{-1/2}\\eta(x)\n \\tag{B1}\n\\]\">\\[\n W:\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\n          \\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu&#x27;,\n \\qquad (W\\eta)(x)=r(x)^{-1/2}\\eta(x)\n \\tag{B1}\n\\]<\/span>\nis unitary: integration of its squared norm cancels the factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span>, and multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{1/2}\\)\">\\(r^{1/2}\\)<\/span> is its inverse. No boundedness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1}\\)\">\\(r^{-1}\\)<\/span> is needed. The formula itself gives the exact square-integrable domains.<\/p>\n<p>For each fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span>, testing against bounded nonnegative Borel <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_X f(y)\\,d(T_g)_*\\mu&#x27;(y)\n &amp;=\\int_X f(T_gx)r(x)\\,d\\mu(x)\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_X f(y)\\,r(T_g^{-1}y)j_g(y)\\,d\\mu(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n \\int_X f(y)\\,d(T_g)_*\\mu&#x27;(y)\n &amp;=\\int_X f(T_gx)r(x)\\,d\\mu(x)\\\\\n &amp;=\\int_X f(y)\\,r(T_g^{-1}y)j_g(y)\\,d\\mu(y).\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe scalar Radon–Nikodym uniqueness proved in the\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#a-finite-radon-nikodym-density-from-hilbert-representation\">finite density theorem<\/a>\ntherefore yields\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n j&#x27;_g(y)=j_g(y)\\frac{r(T_g^{-1}y)}{r(y)}\n \\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n \\tag{B2}\n\\]\">\\[\n j&#x27;_g(y)=j_g(y)\\frac{r(T_g^{-1}y)}{r(y)}\n \\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n \\tag{B2}\n\\]<\/span>\nSubstituting (B1) into (T4) shows\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (WU_gW^{-1}\\zeta)(y)\n   =j&#x27;_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\zeta(T_g^{-1}y).\n \\tag{B3}\n\\]\">\\[\n (WU_gW^{-1}\\zeta)(y)\n   =j&#x27;_g(y)^{1/2}u(g,T_g^{-1}y)\\zeta(T_g^{-1}y).\n \\tag{B3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe <strong>same<\/strong> fibre arrows work in the new probability coordinates. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1/2}\\)\">\\(r^{-1/2}\\)<\/span> is real and positive, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> also intertwines the conjugations, the cones and every decomposable algebra element. Essential boundedness and equality of fields are unchanged because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mu&#x27;\\sim\\mu\\)\">\\(\\mu&#x27;\\sim\\mu\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For a positive normal functional <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\psi\\)\">\\(\\psi\\)<\/span>, its representing section in the new Hilbert coordinates is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi(x)\\)\">\\(r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi(x)\\)<\/span>. Its central mass and its normalized conditional vector obey\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n h&#x27;_\\psi=\\frac{h_\\psi}{r},\\qquad\n (h&#x27;_\\psi)^{-1/2}r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n       =h_\\psi^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n \\quad\\text{where }h_\\psi&gt;0.\n \\tag{B4}\n\\]\">\\[\n h&#x27;_\\psi=\\frac{h_\\psi}{r},\\qquad\n (h&#x27;_\\psi)^{-1/2}r^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n       =h_\\psi^{-1/2}\\xi_\\psi\n \\quad\\text{where }h_\\psi&gt;0.\n \\tag{B4}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus changing the reference probability changes both the raw section and its mass, but their normalized cone vector is unchanged. This includes using another faithful normal state as the reference: its restriction to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is an equivalent probability, and its own conditional states have their cone vectors in these same standard-form fibres.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L41.md","start_offset":60013,"end_offset":62352,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"60d2642508937f6cf6e6473d532a50b36afd3d1ef5461444fd5c48d508c6f6f4":{"markdown":"<a id=\"l31-7\"><\/a>\n## 7. Finite spectral domains and Haar normalization\n\nFor every $f\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)$, the complete finite-domain statement is\n\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta},\\qquad\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M.\n \\tag{L31.7.a}\n\\]\nFirst suppose $f\\ge0$. The bounded function $h=\\sqrt f$ satisfies\n\\[\n E_\\beta(J(h)^*J(h))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M\\in M_+.\n \\tag{L31.7.b}\n\\]\nConsequently $J(h)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}$ and $J(f)=J(h)^*J(h)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}$. The finite extension agrees there with the positive weight, proving (L31.7.a). For complex $f$, each positive or negative part of its real or imaginary part is bounded and integrable, because it is at most $|f|$. Decompose $f$ into these four positive functions and use the linearity of $J$ and $\\dot E_\\beta$. This proves membership as well as the integral formula, with no difference of infinite values.\n\nThe spectral part of the quadratic domain has the exact description\n\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad f\\in L^2(H)\n \\qquad(f\\in L^\\infty(H)),\n \\qquad\n E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\left(\\int_H|f|^2\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b1}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(J\\) is a star homomorphism, so \\(J(f)^*J(f)=J(|f|^2)\\). Apply (L31.6.a) to the bounded positive function \\(|f|^2\\). Its output is bounded precisely when the scalar integral is finite, since \\(M\\ne0\\). This proves both directions, on the entire spectral algebra.\n\nFor bounded \\(f,g\\in L^2(H)\\), the inequality \\(2|\\overline f g|\\le |f|^2+|g|^2\\) shows that \\(\\overline f g\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)\\). Equation (L31.7.a) therefore gives the finite pairing\n\\[\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(g))\n       =\\left(\\int_H\\overline f\\,g\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b2}\n\\]\nThe product belongs to the full finite linear domain by its definition in (L31.4.g), as well as by the integrability argument. All these conclusions hold without a countable Haar exhaustion.\n\n**Finite square, infinite average.** Suppose \\(H=\\mathbb R\\) with Haar measure \\(dp/(2\\pi)\\), and put \\(f(p)=(1+|p|)^{-1}\\). This is a bounded positive function. By the [scalar fundamental theorem](OA-FLOW-SC.md#sc-08), applied on each half of the interval,\n\\[\n \\int_{-R}^{R}f(p)^2\\,\\frac{dp}{2\\pi}\n     =\\frac1\\pi\\left(1-\\frac1{1+R}\\right)\n     \\quad(R>0).\n \\tag{L31.7.b3}\n\\]\nFor example, the primitive on the positive half-line is \\(-(1+p)^{-1}\\). Increasing the compact intervals and using [the compact-test identity](#l31-1) gives \\(\\|f\\|_2^2=1/\\pi\\). On each interval \\([2^n,2^{n+1})\\), \\(n\\ge0\\), the integral of \\(f\\) with respect to \\(dp\\) is at least \\(2^n/(1+2^{n+1})\\ge1/3\\). Finite unions of these disjoint intervals have arbitrarily large integrals, so \\(\\int_H f\\,d\\chi=\\infty\\). Consequently \\(J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\\), with \\(E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\pi^{-1}1_M\\), whereas \\(E_\\beta(J(f))=\\infty1_M\\). Since \\(J(f)\\) is positive, (L31.4.i) also gives \\(J(f)\\notin\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}\\). A finite quadratic value thus need not give a finite average of the positive operator itself.\n\nTaking $f=1$ in (L31.6.a) gives\n\\[\n E_\\beta(1)=|H|1_M,\\qquad |H|:=\\int_H1\\,d\\chi.\n \\tag{L31.7.c}\n\\]\nThe total Haar mass is finite precisely when $H$ is compact. Indeed, a compact group has finite positive mass. If $H$ is noncompact, choose a relatively compact nonempty open set $V$. Recursively choose $t_j\\in H$ outside the finite union of the compact sets $t_i\\overline V\\,\\overline V^{-1}$ already forbidden. Then the sets $t_jV$ are pairwise disjoint and have the same strictly positive measure. Their finite unions have arbitrarily large mass, so $|H|=\\infty$.\n\nIn our dual pair, $H$ is compact precisely when $G$ is discrete. For clarity, if $G$ is discrete, every algebraic character is continuous, and the character equations define $\\widehat G$ as a closed subgroup of the compact product $\\mathbb T^G$. The compact-open topology is the topology of pointwise convergence, since compact subsets of a discrete group are finite. Conversely, for a compact abelian group $H$ the compact-open neighborhood\n\\[\n \\left\\{\\chi\\in\\widehat H:\n          \\sup_{t\\in H}|\\chi(t)-1|<1\\right\\}\n \\tag{L31.7.d}\n\\]\ncontains only the trivial character: any nontrivial subgroup of $\\mathbb T$ has an element at distance at least $1$ from $1$, as one sees by taking a suitable power of a nontrivial element. Hence $\\widehat H$ is discrete, and topological biduality identifies it with $G$. These are the compactness and biduality arguments in [Haar mass and the discrete endpoint](OA-FLOW-DA.md#da-haar). Since $N\\ne0$, also $M\\ne0$, so (L31.7.c) is a bounded value exactly when $G$ is discrete.\n\nSuppose now that $G$ is discrete and its Haar measure assigns mass $c>0$ to each singleton. The positive Fourier transform of $1_{\\{0\\}}$ is the constant function $c$. Plancherel therefore gives\n\\[\n c=\\|1_{\\{0\\}}\\|_{L^2(G)}^2\n   =\\|c1_H\\|_{L^2(H)}^2=c^2|H|,\n \\qquad |H|=c^{-1}.\n \\tag{L31.7.e}\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\n \\mathcal E=c\\dot E_\\beta:N\\longrightarrow M\n \\tag{L31.7.f}\n\\]\nis a normal faithful conditional expectation. Here the finite extension is defined on all of $N$: for $x\\in N_+$,\n\\[\n 0\\le E_\\beta(x)\\le\\|x\\|E_\\beta(1)\n       =c^{-1}\\|x\\|1_M,\n \\tag{L31.7.g}\n\\]\nso every positive element has bounded weight, and (L31.4.i) gives $\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}=N$. The map $\\mathcal E$ is positive and unital, is normal and faithful, and is an $M$-bimodule map by Section 4. Since $\\beta$ fixes $M$ pointwise, the integral formula gives $\\mathcal E(a)=a$ for $a\\in M$, so its range is exactly $M$ and it is idempotent. It is contractive as well: $H$ is compact, and the inclusion of $\\mathcal E$ in $N$ is the average $c\\int_H\\beta_\\eta(\\,\\cdot\\,)\\,d\\eta$ with total scalar mass one. Evaluation at a normal functional bounds its norm by one. In particular, counting measure on $G$ gives $c=1$ and $E_\\beta$ itself is the conditional expectation.\n\nFinally consider changes of Haar scale. If only $d\\chi$ is replaced by $b\\,d\\chi$, $b>0$, the actual action average becomes $bE_\\beta$, by (L31.5.b) and injectivity of cone inclusion. With $ds$ held fixed, the new dual measure is Plancherel-compatible only when $b=1$: the squared Fourier norm of any nonzero compact continuous function is multiplied by $b$, whereas its squared norm on $G$ is unchanged.\n\nIf instead $ds'=a\\,ds$ and compatibility is retained, then\n\\[\n d\\chi'=a^{-1}d\\chi,\\qquad\n E_\\beta'=a^{-1}E_\\beta.\n \\tag{L31.7.h}\n\\]\nThe comparison of crossed products is the normal isomorphism preserving the named coefficient and group generators. Equivalently, conjugate the regular representations by multiplication by $\\sqrt a$ on their $G$ variable; this intertwines both $\\pi_\\alpha$ and $\\lambda_s$. After recognition, both models are the same $N$, and the whole-average identity proves the second equality in (L31.7.h). The Haar coordinate $J$ is unchanged: the measure class of $L^\\infty(H)$ is unchanged, and its values $J(e_s)=u_s$ determine it uniquely by [the normal-coordinate construction](#l31-2).\n\nThe Fourier operators verify the reciprocal constant directly. On compact continuous vectors $\\mathcal F_+'=a\\mathcal F_+$, because the source Haar measure was multiplied by $a$. The unitaries from the rescaled Hilbert spaces to the original ones are\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n V_G:L^2(G,a\\,ds)&\\longrightarrow L^2(G,ds),\n       &V_G\\xi&=\\sqrt a\\,\\xi,\\\\\n V_H:L^2(H,a^{-1}d\\chi)&\\longrightarrow L^2(H,d\\chi),\n       &V_H\\eta&=a^{-1/2}\\eta.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L31.7.i}\n\\]\nThus, first on compact continuous vectors and then by density,\n\\[\n V_H\\mathcal F_+'=\\mathcal F_+V_G.\n \\tag{L31.7.j}\n\\]\nThe norm calculation also forces the reciprocal Haar scale: if $d\\chi'=b\\,d\\chi$, unitarity of $a\\mathcal F_+$ from $L^2(G,a\\,ds)$ requires $a^2b=a$, hence $b=a^{-1}$. There is no group-coordinate inversion in this comparison. At the discrete endpoint $c'=ac$, the normalized expectation is unchanged, since $c'E_\\beta'=cE_\\beta$.","html":"<h2>7. Finite spectral domains and Haar normalization<\/h2>\n<p>For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)\\)\">\\(f\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)\\)<\/span>, the complete finite-domain statement is\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta},\\qquad\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M.\n \\tag{L31.7.a}\n\\]\">\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta},\\qquad\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M.\n \\tag{L31.7.a}\n\\]<\/span>\nFirst suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\ge0\\)\">\\(f\\ge0\\)<\/span>. The bounded function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h=\\sqrt f\\)\">\\(h=\\sqrt f\\)<\/span> satisfies\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n E_\\beta(J(h)^*J(h))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M\\in M_+.\n \\tag{L31.7.b}\n\\]\">\\[\n E_\\beta(J(h)^*J(h))=\\left(\\int_Hf\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M\\in M_+.\n \\tag{L31.7.b}\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(h)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\\)\">\\(J(h)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(f)=J(h)^*J(h)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}\\)\">\\(J(f)=J(h)^*J(h)\\in\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}\\)<\/span>. The finite extension agrees there with the positive weight, proving (L31.7.a). For complex <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>, each positive or negative part of its real or imaginary part is bounded and integrable, because it is at most <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|f|\\)\">\\(|f|\\)<\/span>. Decompose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> into these four positive functions and use the linearity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\dot E_\\beta\\)\">\\(\\dot E_\\beta\\)<\/span>. This proves membership as well as the integral formula, with no difference of infinite values.<\/p>\n<p>The spectral part of the quadratic domain has the exact description\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad f\\in L^2(H)\n \\qquad(f\\in L^\\infty(H)),\n \\qquad\n E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\left(\\int_H|f|^2\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b1}\n\\]\">\\[\n J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad f\\in L^2(H)\n \\qquad(f\\in L^\\infty(H)),\n \\qquad\n E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\left(\\int_H|f|^2\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b1}\n\\]<\/span>\nIndeed, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> is a star homomorphism, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(f)^*J(f)=J(|f|^2)\\)\">\\(J(f)^*J(f)=J(|f|^2)\\)<\/span>. Apply (L31.6.a) to the bounded positive function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|f|^2\\)\">\\(|f|^2\\)<\/span>. Its output is bounded precisely when the scalar integral is finite, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span>. This proves both directions, on the entire spectral algebra.<\/p>\n<p>For bounded <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f,g\\in L^2(H)\\)\">\\(f,g\\in L^2(H)\\)<\/span>, the inequality <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2|\\overline f g|\\le |f|^2+|g|^2\\)\">\\(2|\\overline f g|\\le |f|^2+|g|^2\\)<\/span> shows that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\overline f g\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)\\)\">\\(\\overline f g\\in L^1(H)\\cap L^\\infty(H)\\)<\/span>. Equation (L31.7.a) therefore gives the finite pairing\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(g))\n       =\\left(\\int_H\\overline f\\,g\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b2}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\dot E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(g))\n       =\\left(\\int_H\\overline f\\,g\\,d\\chi\\right)1_M .\n \\tag{L31.7.b2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe product belongs to the full finite linear domain by its definition in (L31.4.g), as well as by the integrability argument. All these conclusions hold without a countable Haar exhaustion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Finite square, infinite average.<\/strong> Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(H=\\mathbb R\\)<\/span> with Haar measure <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dp/(2\\pi)\\)\">\\(dp/(2\\pi)\\)<\/span>, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(p)=(1+|p|)^{-1}\\)\">\\(f(p)=(1+|p|)^{-1}\\)<\/span>. This is a bounded positive function. By the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-SC.html#sc-08\">scalar fundamental theorem<\/a>, applied on each half of the interval,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\int_{-R}^{R}f(p)^2\\,\\frac{dp}{2\\pi}\n     =\\frac1\\pi\\left(1-\\frac1{1+R}\\right)\n     \\quad(R&gt;0).\n \\tag{L31.7.b3}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\int_{-R}^{R}f(p)^2\\,\\frac{dp}{2\\pi}\n     =\\frac1\\pi\\left(1-\\frac1{1+R}\\right)\n     \\quad(R&gt;0).\n \\tag{L31.7.b3}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor example, the primitive on the positive half-line is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-(1+p)^{-1}\\)\">\\(-(1+p)^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Increasing the compact intervals and using <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l31-1\">the compact-test identity<\/a> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|f\\|_2^2=1/\\pi\\)\">\\(\\|f\\|_2^2=1/\\pi\\)<\/span>. On each interval <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([2^n,2^{n+1})\\)\">\\([2^n,2^{n+1})\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\ge0\\)\">\\(n\\ge0\\)<\/span>, the integral of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span> with respect to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(dp\\)\">\\(dp\\)<\/span> is at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2^n/(1+2^{n+1})\\ge1/3\\)\">\\(2^n/(1+2^{n+1})\\ge1/3\\)<\/span>. Finite unions of these disjoint intervals have arbitrarily large integrals, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int_H f\\,d\\chi=\\infty\\)\">\\(\\int_H f\\,d\\chi=\\infty\\)<\/span>. Consequently <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\\)\">\\(J(f)\\in\\mathfrak n_{E_\\beta}\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\pi^{-1}1_M\\)\">\\(E_\\beta(J(f)^*J(f))=\\pi^{-1}1_M\\)<\/span>, whereas <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_\\beta(J(f))=\\infty1_M\\)\">\\(E_\\beta(J(f))=\\infty1_M\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(f)\\)\">\\(J(f)\\)<\/span> is positive, (L31.4.i) also gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(f)\\notin\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}\\)\">\\(J(f)\\notin\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}\\)<\/span>. A finite quadratic value thus need not give a finite average of the positive operator itself.<\/p>\n<p>Taking <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f=1\\)\">\\(f=1\\)<\/span> in (L31.6.a) gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n E_\\beta(1)=|H|1_M,\\qquad |H|:=\\int_H1\\,d\\chi.\n \\tag{L31.7.c}\n\\]\">\\[\n E_\\beta(1)=|H|1_M,\\qquad |H|:=\\int_H1\\,d\\chi.\n \\tag{L31.7.c}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe total Haar mass is finite precisely when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is compact. Indeed, a compact group has finite positive mass. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is noncompact, choose a relatively compact nonempty open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V\\)\">\\(V\\)<\/span>. Recursively choose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_j\\in H\\)\">\\(t_j\\in H\\)<\/span> outside the finite union of the compact sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_i\\overline V\\,\\overline V^{-1}\\)\">\\(t_i\\overline V\\,\\overline V^{-1}\\)<\/span> already forbidden. Then the sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t_jV\\)\">\\(t_jV\\)<\/span> are pairwise disjoint and have the same strictly positive measure. Their finite unions have arbitrarily large mass, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(|H|=\\infty\\)\">\\(|H|=\\infty\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>In our dual pair, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is compact precisely when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is discrete. For clarity, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is discrete, every algebraic character is continuous, and the character equations define <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat G\\)\">\\(\\widehat G\\)<\/span> as a closed subgroup of the compact product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb T^G\\)\">\\(\\mathbb T^G\\)<\/span>. The compact-open topology is the topology of pointwise convergence, since compact subsets of a discrete group are finite. Conversely, for a compact abelian group <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> the compact-open neighborhood\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\left\\{\\chi\\in\\widehat H:\n          \\sup_{t\\in H}|\\chi(t)-1|&lt;1\\right\\}\n \\tag{L31.7.d}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\left\\{\\chi\\in\\widehat H:\n          \\sup_{t\\in H}|\\chi(t)-1|&lt;1\\right\\}\n \\tag{L31.7.d}\n\\]<\/span>\ncontains only the trivial character: any nontrivial subgroup of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(\\mathbb T\\)<\/span> has an element at distance at least <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, as one sees by taking a suitable power of a nontrivial element. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\widehat H\\)\">\\(\\widehat H\\)<\/span> is discrete, and topological biduality identifies it with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>. These are the compactness and biduality arguments in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-DA.html#da-haar\">Haar mass and the discrete endpoint<\/a>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\ne0\\)\">\\(N\\ne0\\)<\/span>, also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span>, so (L31.7.c) is a bounded value exactly when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is discrete.<\/p>\n<p>Suppose now that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is discrete and its Haar measure assigns mass <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&gt;0\\)\">\\(c&gt;0\\)<\/span> to each singleton. The positive Fourier transform of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1_{\\{0\\}}\\)\">\\(1_{\\{0\\}}\\)<\/span> is the constant function <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\)\">\\(c\\)<\/span>. Plancherel therefore gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c=\\|1_{\\{0\\}}\\|_{L^2(G)}^2\n   =\\|c1_H\\|_{L^2(H)}^2=c^2|H|,\n \\qquad |H|=c^{-1}.\n \\tag{L31.7.e}\n\\]\">\\[\n c=\\|1_{\\{0\\}}\\|_{L^2(G)}^2\n   =\\|c1_H\\|_{L^2(H)}^2=c^2|H|,\n \\qquad |H|=c^{-1}.\n \\tag{L31.7.e}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt follows that\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\mathcal E=c\\dot E_\\beta:N\\longrightarrow M\n \\tag{L31.7.f}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\mathcal E=c\\dot E_\\beta:N\\longrightarrow M\n \\tag{L31.7.f}\n\\]<\/span>\nis a normal faithful conditional expectation. Here the finite extension is defined on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>: for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in N_+\\)\">\\(x\\in N_+\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n 0\\le E_\\beta(x)\\le\\|x\\|E_\\beta(1)\n       =c^{-1}\\|x\\|1_M,\n \\tag{L31.7.g}\n\\]\">\\[\n 0\\le E_\\beta(x)\\le\\|x\\|E_\\beta(1)\n       =c^{-1}\\|x\\|1_M,\n \\tag{L31.7.g}\n\\]<\/span>\nso every positive element has bounded weight, and (L31.4.i) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}=N\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak m_{E_\\beta}=N\\)<\/span>. The map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E\\)<\/span> is positive and unital, is normal and faithful, and is an <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span>-bimodule map by Section 4. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\)\">\\(\\beta\\)<\/span> fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> pointwise, the integral formula gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E(a)=a\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E(a)=a\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M\\)\">\\(a\\in M\\)<\/span>, so its range is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> and it is idempotent. It is contractive as well: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> is compact, and the inclusion of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal E\\)\">\\(\\mathcal E\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> is the average <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c\\int_H\\beta_\\eta(\\,\\cdot\\,)\\,d\\eta\\)\">\\(c\\int_H\\beta_\\eta(\\,\\cdot\\,)\\,d\\eta\\)<\/span> with total scalar mass one. Evaluation at a normal functional bounds its norm by one. In particular, counting measure on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c=1\\)\">\\(c=1\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E_\\beta\\)\">\\(E_\\beta\\)<\/span> itself is the conditional expectation.<\/p>\n<p>Finally consider changes of Haar scale. If only <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\chi\\)\">\\(d\\chi\\)<\/span> is replaced by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\,d\\chi\\)\">\\(b\\,d\\chi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&gt;0\\)\">\\(b&gt;0\\)<\/span>, the actual action average becomes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(bE_\\beta\\)\">\\(bE_\\beta\\)<\/span>, by (L31.5.b) and injectivity of cone inclusion. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ds\\)\">\\(ds\\)<\/span> held fixed, the new dual measure is Plancherel-compatible only when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=1\\)\">\\(b=1\\)<\/span>: the squared Fourier norm of any nonzero compact continuous function is multiplied by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, whereas its squared norm on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> is unchanged.<\/p>\n<p>If instead <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(ds&#x27;=a\\,ds\\)\">\\(ds&#x27;=a\\,ds\\)<\/span> and compatibility is retained, then\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n d\\chi&#x27;=a^{-1}d\\chi,\\qquad\n E_\\beta&#x27;=a^{-1}E_\\beta.\n \\tag{L31.7.h}\n\\]\">\\[\n d\\chi&#x27;=a^{-1}d\\chi,\\qquad\n E_\\beta&#x27;=a^{-1}E_\\beta.\n \\tag{L31.7.h}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe comparison of crossed products is the normal isomorphism preserving the named coefficient and group generators. Equivalently, conjugate the regular representations by multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sqrt a\\)\">\\(\\sqrt a\\)<\/span> on their <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span> variable; this intertwines both <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi_\\alpha\\)\">\\(\\pi_\\alpha\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_s\\)\">\\(\\lambda_s\\)<\/span>. After recognition, both models are the same <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>, and the whole-average identity proves the second equality in (L31.7.h). The Haar coordinate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> is unchanged: the measure class of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^\\infty(H)\\)\">\\(L^\\infty(H)\\)<\/span> is unchanged, and its values <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J(e_s)=u_s\\)\">\\(J(e_s)=u_s\\)<\/span> determine it uniquely by <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#l31-2\">the normal-coordinate construction<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>The Fourier operators verify the reciprocal constant directly. On compact continuous vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal F_+&#x27;=a\\mathcal F_+\\)\">\\(\\mathcal F_+&#x27;=a\\mathcal F_+\\)<\/span>, because the source Haar measure was multiplied by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span>. The unitaries from the rescaled Hilbert spaces to the original ones are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n V_G:L^2(G,a\\,ds)&amp;\\longrightarrow L^2(G,ds),\n       &amp;V_G\\xi&amp;=\\sqrt a\\,\\xi,\\\\\n V_H:L^2(H,a^{-1}d\\chi)&amp;\\longrightarrow L^2(H,d\\chi),\n       &amp;V_H\\eta&amp;=a^{-1/2}\\eta.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L31.7.i}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n V_G:L^2(G,a\\,ds)&amp;\\longrightarrow L^2(G,ds),\n       &amp;V_G\\xi&amp;=\\sqrt a\\,\\xi,\\\\\n V_H:L^2(H,a^{-1}d\\chi)&amp;\\longrightarrow L^2(H,d\\chi),\n       &amp;V_H\\eta&amp;=a^{-1/2}\\eta.\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{L31.7.i}\n\\]<\/span>\nThus, first on compact continuous vectors and then by density,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n V_H\\mathcal F_+&#x27;=\\mathcal F_+V_G.\n \\tag{L31.7.j}\n\\]\">\\[\n V_H\\mathcal F_+&#x27;=\\mathcal F_+V_G.\n \\tag{L31.7.j}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe norm calculation also forces the reciprocal Haar scale: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\chi&#x27;=b\\,d\\chi\\)\">\\(d\\chi&#x27;=b\\,d\\chi\\)<\/span>, unitarity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\mathcal F_+\\)\">\\(a\\mathcal F_+\\)<\/span> from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(G,a\\,ds)\\)\">\\(L^2(G,a\\,ds)\\)<\/span> requires <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a^2b=a\\)\">\\(a^2b=a\\)<\/span>, hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=a^{-1}\\)\">\\(b=a^{-1}\\)<\/span>. There is no group-coordinate inversion in this comparison. At the discrete endpoint <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&#x27;=ac\\)\">\\(c&#x27;=ac\\)<\/span>, the normalized expectation is unchanged, since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c&#x27;E_\\beta&#x27;=cE_\\beta\\)\">\\(c&#x27;E_\\beta&#x27;=cE_\\beta\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L31.md","start_offset":33859,"end_offset":41748,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"60372b0b77b2beeee07a8ab80e83743d50ea3e4363d99d56e9bb5f2a368e04d8":{"markdown":"<a id=\"kt-6\"><\/a>\n## 6. All choices give normally isomorphic stages\n\nWe record actual maps, including their normal inverses. On a fixed algebra \\(P\\), let\n\\[\n c_t=(D\\psi:D\\varphi)_t.\n\\]\nThe normalized cocycle equations give\n\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=\\operatorname{Ad}(c_t)\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\) and\n\\(c_{t+s}=c_t\\sigma_t^\\varphi(c_s)\\).\nIn a common faithful normal representation of \\(P\\), define\n\\[\n [W\\xi](r)=c_{-r}^*\\xi(r)\n \\quad\\text{on }L^2(\\mathbb R,H).\n \\tag{KT27}\n\\]\nThe field and its adjoint act as inverse unitaries. Strong continuity verifies their definition on compact tensors, and density extends them to the whole Hilbert space. Direct computation gives\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\pi_\\psi(x)W^*&=\\pi_\\varphi(x),\\\\\n W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*&=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t)\\lambda_\\varphi(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{KT28}\n\\]\nFor the second equation the multiplier is\n\\(c_{-r}^*c_{t-r}=\\sigma^\\varphi_{-r}(c_t)\\), in that order.\n\nThe image contains all coefficients and all\n\\(\\lambda_\\varphi(t)=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t^*)W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*\\).\nIt is therefore onto. Unitary conjugation supplies both normality and the normal inverse. The [regular representation-independence theorem NR4](OA-FLOW-NR.md#oa-flow.nr.4) transports these generator formulas to any other faithful normal realizations. The multiplication \\(e^{-isr}\\) implementing the dual action commutes with \\(W\\), so the map restricts to a normal conjugacy of the full centers and their flows.\n\nFor three weights the [ordered chain law in CORE5](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-5) gives\n\\[\n J_{\\psi,\\varphi}J_{\\omega,\\psi}=J_{\\omega,\\varphi},\n \\qquad J_{\\psi,\\varphi}^{-1}=J_{\\varphi,\\psi}.\n \\tag{KT29}\n\\]\nThese are equalities on the whole normal algebras, since their ordered generator formulas agree on the generated star algebra.\n\nA normal flow conjugacy \\(f:(C,\\theta)\\to(D,\\eta)\\) has an equally concrete crossed-product extension:\n\\[\n \\widetilde f(\\pi_C(x))=\\pi_D(f(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde f(\\lambda_C(t))=\\lambda_D(t).\n \\tag{KT30}\n\\]\nRepresent \\(D\\) faithfully on \\(H\\) and \\(C\\) through that representation composed with \\(f\\). On \\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\), the two coefficient fields agree because\n\\(f(\\theta_{-r}x)=\\eta_{-r}(f(x))\\), and the translations agree. This identifies the two entire regular algebras. It proves normality and onto-ness, and the same construction for \\(f^{-1}\\) is the normal inverse. NR4 handles a change from these convenient representations to any chosen ones.\n\nFinally, if \\(g:P\\to Q\\) is a normal isomorphism, transport \\(\\varphi\\) to \\(\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}\\). Its GNS unitary is\n\\(\\Lambda_\\varphi(x)\\mapsto\\Lambda_{\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}}(g(x))\\).\nEquality of the entire weights makes it an onto isometry; it intertwines the closed Tomita operators and hence the modular groups. The regular construction just used extends \\(g\\) to the cores and their dual flows. This is [CORE8's normal functor](OA-FLOW-CORE.md#core-8), with inverse induced by \\(g^{-1}\\).\n\nInductively apply these maps to (KT2). At each finite stage they transport the full chosen weight, the core center, its action, and the next regular crossed product. Thus all presentations give the same type at that stage and normally isomorphic reached objects.","html":"<h2>6. All choices give normally isomorphic stages<\/h2>\n<p>We record actual maps, including their normal inverses. On a fixed algebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, let\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n c_t=(D\\psi:D\\varphi)_t.\n\\]\">\\[\n c_t=(D\\psi:D\\varphi)_t.\n\\]<\/span>\nThe normalized cocycle equations give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=\\operatorname{Ad}(c_t)\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=\\operatorname{Ad}(c_t)\\sigma_t^\\varphi\\)<\/span> and\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_{t+s}=c_t\\sigma_t^\\varphi(c_s)\\)\">\\(c_{t+s}=c_t\\sigma_t^\\varphi(c_s)\\)<\/span>.\nIn a common faithful normal representation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>, define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n [W\\xi](r)=c_{-r}^*\\xi(r)\n \\quad\\text{on }L^2(\\mathbb R,H).\n \\tag{KT27}\n\\]\">\\[\n [W\\xi](r)=c_{-r}^*\\xi(r)\n \\quad\\text{on }L^2(\\mathbb R,H).\n \\tag{KT27}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe field and its adjoint act as inverse unitaries. Strong continuity verifies their definition on compact tensors, and density extends them to the whole Hilbert space. Direct computation gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\pi_\\psi(x)W^*&amp;=\\pi_\\varphi(x),\\\\\n W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*&amp;=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t)\\lambda_\\varphi(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{KT28}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n W\\pi_\\psi(x)W^*&amp;=\\pi_\\varphi(x),\\\\\n W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*&amp;=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t)\\lambda_\\varphi(t).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{KT28}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor the second equation the multiplier is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_{-r}^*c_{t-r}=\\sigma^\\varphi_{-r}(c_t)\\)\">\\(c_{-r}^*c_{t-r}=\\sigma^\\varphi_{-r}(c_t)\\)<\/span>, in that order.<\/p>\n<p>The image contains all coefficients and all\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\lambda_\\varphi(t)=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t^*)W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*\\)\">\\(\\lambda_\\varphi(t)=\\pi_\\varphi(c_t^*)W\\lambda_\\psi(t)W^*\\)<\/span>.\nIt is therefore onto. Unitary conjugation supplies both normality and the normal inverse. The <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-NR.html#oa-flow.nr.4\">regular representation-independence theorem NR4<\/a> transports these generator formulas to any other faithful normal realizations. The multiplication <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-isr}\\)\">\\(e^{-isr}\\)<\/span> implementing the dual action commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>, so the map restricts to a normal conjugacy of the full centers and their flows.<\/p>\n<p>For three weights the <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-5\">ordered chain law in CORE5<\/a> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_{\\psi,\\varphi}J_{\\omega,\\psi}=J_{\\omega,\\varphi},\n \\qquad J_{\\psi,\\varphi}^{-1}=J_{\\varphi,\\psi}.\n \\tag{KT29}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_{\\psi,\\varphi}J_{\\omega,\\psi}=J_{\\omega,\\varphi},\n \\qquad J_{\\psi,\\varphi}^{-1}=J_{\\varphi,\\psi}.\n \\tag{KT29}\n\\]<\/span>\nThese are equalities on the whole normal algebras, since their ordered generator formulas agree on the generated star algebra.<\/p>\n<p>A normal flow conjugacy <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f:(C,\\theta)\\to(D,\\eta)\\)\">\\(f:(C,\\theta)\\to(D,\\eta)\\)<\/span> has an equally concrete crossed-product extension:\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\widetilde f(\\pi_C(x))=\\pi_D(f(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde f(\\lambda_C(t))=\\lambda_D(t).\n \\tag{KT30}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\widetilde f(\\pi_C(x))=\\pi_D(f(x)),\\qquad\n \\widetilde f(\\lambda_C(t))=\\lambda_D(t).\n \\tag{KT30}\n\\]<\/span>\nRepresent <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> faithfully on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> through that representation composed with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f\\)\">\\(f\\)<\/span>. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)\">\\(L^2(\\mathbb R,H)\\)<\/span>, the two coefficient fields agree because\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f(\\theta_{-r}x)=\\eta_{-r}(f(x))\\)\">\\(f(\\theta_{-r}x)=\\eta_{-r}(f(x))\\)<\/span>, and the translations agree. This identifies the two entire regular algebras. It proves normality and onto-ness, and the same construction for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f^{-1}\\)\">\\(f^{-1}\\)<\/span> is the normal inverse. NR4 handles a change from these convenient representations to any chosen ones.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g:P\\to Q\\)\">\\(g:P\\to Q\\)<\/span> is a normal isomorphism, transport <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}\\)\">\\(\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}\\)<\/span>. Its GNS unitary is\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\varphi(x)\\mapsto\\Lambda_{\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}}(g(x))\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\varphi(x)\\mapsto\\Lambda_{\\varphi\\circ g^{-1}}(g(x))\\)<\/span>.\nEquality of the entire weights makes it an onto isometry; it intertwines the closed Tomita operators and hence the modular groups. The regular construction just used extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\)\">\\(g\\)<\/span> to the cores and their dual flows. This is <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CORE.html#core-8\">CORE8's normal functor<\/a>, with inverse induced by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g^{-1}\\)\">\\(g^{-1}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Inductively apply these maps to (KT2). At each finite stage they transport the full chosen weight, the core center, its action, and the next regular crossed product. Thus all presentations give the same type at that stage and normally isomorphic reached objects.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-KRT.md","start_offset":17681,"end_offset":20849,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"60daa0fc55eae3d7f7604254a26c7debc0d53659e1d17c204b8af09f63d9f0cf":{"markdown":"### Atomic and diffuse regions\n\n**Proposition 6.5** (Atomic and diffuse parts). Let \\(\\Omega\\) be stonean, let \\(I\\) be the set of its isolated points, and put \\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\) and \\(\\Omega_c=\\Omega\\setminus\\Omega_d\\).\n\n1. \\(\\Omega_d\\) and \\(\\Omega_c\\) are clopen. \\(I\\) is an open discrete subset of \\(\\Omega_d\\) that is dense in \\(\\Omega_d\\), and \\(\\Omega_c\\) has no isolated points. The space \\(\\Omega_d\\) is the Stone–Čech compactification of the discrete space \\(I\\), so \\(C(\\Omega_d)\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\).\n2. If \\(\\Omega=A\\cup B\\) is a partition into clopen sets such that some open discrete subset of \\(A\\) is dense in \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) has no isolated points, then \\(A=\\Omega_d\\) and \\(B=\\Omega_c\\).\n3. If \\(\\Omega\\) is hyperstonean, so are \\(\\Omega_d\\) and \\(\\Omega_c\\).\n\nThe clopen hypothesis in (2) is essential, as the complete counterexample in Example 6.6 proves.\n\n**Proof.** (1) \\(I\\) is open, as a union of open points, so \\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\) is clopen, and so is \\(\\Omega_c\\). An isolated point of the open set \\(\\Omega_c\\) would be isolated in \\(\\Omega\\) and so lie in \\(I\\subseteq\\Omega_d\\). The space \\(\\Omega_d\\) is stonean and \\(I\\) is dense in it, so Theorem 4.4 identifies \\(\\Omega_d\\) with \\(\\beta I\\) and \\(C(\\Omega_d)\\) with \\(C_b(I)=\\ell^\\infty(I)\\).\n\n(2) Let \\(D\\) be an open discrete subset of \\(A\\) that is dense in \\(A\\). Each point of \\(D\\) is open in \\(D\\), \\(D\\) is open in \\(A\\), and \\(A\\) is open in \\(\\Omega\\); so \\(D\\subseteq I\\). As \\(A\\) is closed, \\(A=\\overline D\\subseteq\\overline I=\\Omega_d\\). A point of \\(I\\) lying in the open set \\(B\\) would be isolated in \\(B\\), so \\(I\\subseteq A\\), and \\(\\Omega_d\\subseteq A\\) because \\(A\\) is closed.\n\n(3) If \\(\\mathfrak F\\) is a sufficient family of normal measures on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(C\\) is clopen, the restrictions of its members to \\(C\\) form a sufficient family on \\(C\\), by Lemma 6.2. \\(\\square\\)\n\n**Example 6.6** (The parts must be clopen). In \\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\) the isolated points are the points of \\(\\mathbb N\\), so \\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\) and \\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\). The partition into \\(A=\\mathbb N\\) and \\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\) also has the two properties of Proposition 6.5(2), except that the parts are not clopen. Clearly \\(\\mathbb N\\) is open, discrete and dense in itself. To see that \\(B\\) has no isolated points, let \\(p\\in B\\) and let \\(C\\) be a clopen neighbourhood of \\(p\\) in \\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\). The set \\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\) is infinite: otherwise \\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\), which holds because \\(\\mathbb N\\) is dense and \\(C\\) is open, would be a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb N\\) and could not contain \\(p\\). Split \\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\) into two disjoint infinite sets \\(N_1,N_2\\). They are open, so their closures are disjoint (Lemma 4.2(1)) and lie in \\(C\\). The closure of an infinite subset of \\(\\mathbb N\\) is compact and so is not a subset of the discrete set \\(\\mathbb N\\); hence each closure contains a point of \\(B\\). So \\(C\\cap B\\) has at least two points, and \\(p\\) is not isolated in \\(B\\).","html":"<h3>Atomic and diffuse regions<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Proposition 6.5<\/strong> (Atomic and diffuse parts). Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span> be stonean, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> be the set of its isolated points, and put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c=\\Omega\\setminus\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c=\\Omega\\setminus\\Omega_d\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c\\)<\/span> are clopen. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is an open discrete subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> that is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c\\)<\/span> has no isolated points. The space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> is the Stone–Čech compactification of the discrete space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C(\\Omega_d)\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)\">\\(C(\\Omega_d)\\cong\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega=A\\cup B\\)\">\\(\\Omega=A\\cup B\\)<\/span> is a partition into clopen sets such that some open discrete subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> has no isolated points, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(A=\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(B=\\Omega_c\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<li>If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span> is hyperstonean, so are <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c\\)<\/span>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The clopen hypothesis in (2) is essential, as the complete counterexample in Example 6.6 proves.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> (1) <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is open, as a union of open points, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d=\\overline I\\)<\/span> is clopen, and so is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c\\)<\/span>. An isolated point of the open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c\\)<\/span> would be isolated in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span> and so lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\subseteq\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(I\\subseteq\\Omega_d\\)<\/span>. The space <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> is stonean and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> is dense in it, so Theorem 4.4 identifies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta I\\)\">\\(\\beta I\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C(\\Omega_d)\\)\">\\(C(\\Omega_d)\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C_b(I)=\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)\">\\(C_b(I)=\\ell^\\infty(I)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>(2) Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> be an open discrete subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> that is dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. Each point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is open in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> is open in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is open in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span>; so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\subseteq I\\)\">\\(D\\subseteq I\\)<\/span>. As <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is closed, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\overline D\\subseteq\\overline I=\\Omega_d\\)\">\\(A=\\overline D\\subseteq\\overline I=\\Omega_d\\)<\/span>. A point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> lying in the open set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> would be isolated in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\subseteq A\\)\">\\(I\\subseteq A\\)<\/span>, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d\\subseteq A\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d\\subseteq A\\)<\/span> because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> is closed.<\/p>\n<p>(3) If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak F\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak F\\)<\/span> is a sufficient family of normal measures on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> is clopen, the restrictions of its members to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> form a sufficient family on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>, by Lemma 6.2. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Example 6.6<\/strong> (The parts must be clopen). In <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> the isolated points are the points of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\Omega_d=\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\)\">\\(\\Omega_c=\\varnothing\\)<\/span>. The partition into <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A=\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(A=\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(B=\\beta\\mathbb N\\setminus\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> also has the two properties of Proposition 6.5(2), except that the parts are not clopen. Clearly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is open, discrete and dense in itself. To see that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span> has no isolated points, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\in B\\)\">\\(p\\in B\\)<\/span> and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> be a clopen neighbourhood of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\beta\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>. The set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is infinite: otherwise <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\)\">\\(C=\\overline{C\\cap\\mathbb N}\\)<\/span>, which holds because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is dense and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span> is open, would be a finite subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> and could not contain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span>. Split <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(C\\cap\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> into two disjoint infinite sets <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N_1,N_2\\)\">\\(N_1,N_2\\)<\/span>. They are open, so their closures are disjoint (Lemma 4.2(1)) and lie in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\)\">\\(C\\)<\/span>. The closure of an infinite subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span> is compact and so is not a subset of the discrete set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb N\\)\">\\(\\mathbb N\\)<\/span>; hence each closure contains a point of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>. So <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(C\\cap B\\)\">\\(C\\cap B\\)<\/span> has at least two points, and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(p\\)\">\\(p\\)<\/span> is not isolated in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(B\\)\">\\(B\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/foundations-of-von-neumann-algebras/src/abelian-operator-algebras.md","start_offset":95705,"end_offset":98748,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
